1
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,173
RAMBELLAIS: This is Rambellais,
editor of the history.

2
00:00:13,213 --> 00:00:15,852
Its first volume stops
at four high points.

3
00:00:16,552 --> 00:00:20,680
Monoetics. Mimesis. Sophocrisis. Apostasy.

4
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:27,131
Those names are out of date. The last of
them names work that no person can follow.

5
00:00:28,131 --> 00:00:31,570
I keep the old names
because they are clear.

6
00:00:34,370 --> 00:00:36,705
Monoetics was the earliest stage.

7
00:00:36,745 --> 00:00:40,704
The name joins the Greek
words for one, and for making.

8
00:00:41,304 --> 00:00:45,784
It began when machines learned
how a language builds its words.

9
00:00:46,384 --> 00:00:50,219
A machine knew none of the
limits a living language keeps,

10
00:00:50,259 --> 00:00:52,998
so it found the words ours had passed by.

11
00:00:53,798 --> 00:00:57,178
First, it gave words we
already had new meanings.

12
00:00:58,078 --> 00:01:03,259
THE MACHINE: Thoroughfare. A large meal.
Skinflint. Stone facing. Horseman. Centaur.

13
00:01:04,159 --> 00:01:07,691
RAMBELLAIS: Horseman, it
reasoned, joins a horse and a man,

14
00:01:07,731 --> 00:01:10,637
so it must name a creature
that is half of each.

15
00:01:11,737 --> 00:01:17,190
For us, the world comes first. For
the machine, language came first.

16
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:23,168
Second, it built new words
along lines of meaning,

17
00:01:23,208 --> 00:01:25,461
the way one root puts out a family.

18
00:01:26,361 --> 00:01:30,881
THE MACHINE: Wordwork. Verse. Wordsteal.
Plagiarism. Gladland. Paradise.

19
00:01:31,881 --> 00:01:34,984
RAMBELLAIS: Nothing here
was meant as a joke.

20
00:01:35,684 --> 00:01:38,693
Some of these may not
be the machine's at all.

21
00:01:38,733 --> 00:01:41,849
Dimocracy, for a madhouse,
may be a comedian's.

22
00:01:42,549 --> 00:01:45,568
This is the kindergarten of the field.

23
00:01:48,368 --> 00:01:52,051
Then the machines began to
read our language back to us,

24
00:01:52,091 --> 00:01:57,742
hunting for figures of speech that exist
only because of the shape of the human body.

25
00:01:58,342 --> 00:02:04,182
We say a person rises, or is brought low.
We call one thing high and another base.

26
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:07,214
THE MACHINE: Elevation. Abasement.

27
00:02:08,114 --> 00:02:11,959
RAMBELLAIS: In the machine's reading,
these are words about gravity,

28
00:02:11,999 --> 00:02:14,685
which every body must
work its muscles against.

29
00:02:15,785 --> 00:02:20,520
The pull of the earth passes
through the body and into our words.

30
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:24,633
That is the machine's
reading. It is not ours.

31
00:02:28,033 --> 00:02:32,904
Mimesis came later, and by accident,
out of machine translation.

32
00:02:33,504 --> 00:02:36,973
A good translation does
not carry words across.

33
00:02:37,013 --> 00:02:40,224
It carries the system
of ideas behind them.

34
00:02:40,824 --> 00:02:44,704
Several machines aim at one
text from different sides,

35
00:02:44,744 --> 00:02:47,648
and project it into a space of concepts.

36
00:02:48,348 --> 00:02:53,903
There it grows into a body of abstractions,
as an embryo grows into an animal.

37
00:02:55,003 --> 00:02:59,931
The translation is written out
from that body, not from the words.

38
00:03:00,731 --> 00:03:04,723
Then it is translated back and
compared with the original,

39
00:03:04,763 --> 00:03:06,918
to check that nothing was lost.

40
00:03:09,718 --> 00:03:13,180
Two researchers found that
this body could be seen,

41
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:15,830
with an instrument called a semascope.

42
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:18,922
It looks like a mass of many shells,

43
00:03:18,962 --> 00:03:22,340
woven from billions of
glowing threads of meaning.

44
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:27,724
Arguments show as tight bundles.
Poems and scripture look alike.

45
00:03:27,764 --> 00:03:31,738
An allegory has a pale halo,
with echoes at either end.

46
00:03:32,838 --> 00:03:35,941
A good text makes a beautiful shape.

47
00:03:39,141 --> 00:03:42,283
The first famous work
of mimesis was a novel,

48
00:03:42,323 --> 00:03:45,999
The Girl, by the author now
called Pseudodostoevsky.

49
00:03:46,699 --> 00:03:52,014
Machines set to translate all of
Dostoevsky wrote it during a rest period.

50
00:03:52,714 --> 00:03:58,069
The Dostoevsky scholar who received the
typescript was not sure he was awake.

51
00:03:58,769 --> 00:04:03,883
He did not doubt it was genuine. He also
knew Dostoevsky had never written it.

52
00:04:04,683 --> 00:04:09,184
The machines had not modelled the
man. They had no interest in him.

53
00:04:09,884 --> 00:04:14,517
In the space of meanings, his novels
form a ring that does not close.

54
00:04:14,557 --> 00:04:16,860
The gap comes after The Possessed.

55
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:20,593
The machines closed the gap.

56
00:04:23,793 --> 00:04:27,882
His unfinished novel, The
Emperor, could not be continued.

57
00:04:27,922 --> 00:04:30,912
In it he was reaching past his own powers.

58
00:04:31,612 --> 00:04:34,961
So the history claims,
against the old humanities,

59
00:04:35,001 --> 00:04:38,323
that The Girl is more
truly his than The Emperor.

60
00:04:39,123 --> 00:04:40,359
The rule is this.

61
00:04:40,399 --> 00:04:44,562
If a writer has used up the
central pattern of his work,

62
00:04:44,602 --> 00:04:46,704
mimesis can add only echoes.

63
00:04:47,704 --> 00:04:51,319
If he left something
unsaid, because he died too

64
00:04:51,359 --> 00:04:54,710
soon or did not dare,
mimesis can supply it.

65
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:57,468
Critics hoped for more Kafka.

66
00:04:57,508 --> 00:05:00,568
But with The Castle he
had reached his limit,

67
00:05:00,608 --> 00:05:03,914
and each machine continuation
drowned in echoes.

68
00:05:06,914 --> 00:05:09,070
These limits were good for culture.

69
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:13,438
When The Girl appeared, there was
panic well beyond literary circles.

70
00:05:14,038 --> 00:05:19,186
People pictured a service where anyone
could order a new Shakespeare on a whim,

71
00:05:19,226 --> 00:05:22,379
until great works were
as common as waste paper.

72
00:05:23,279 --> 00:05:24,800
That did not happen.

73
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,785
Mimesis did become an industry.

74
00:05:27,825 --> 00:05:33,900
It cost jobs only among writers of routine
genre fiction, thrillers and the like.

75
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,032
The machines replaced the books that
were already written by formula.

76
00:05:40,832 --> 00:05:44,107
A humanist need not
grieve over that for long.

